<!-- COMMON MISTAKES -->
<section class="content" id="common-mistakes" aria-labelledby="mistakes-heading">
  <h2 id="mistakes-heading">Common Pitfalls in WET to EST Conversions</h2>
  <article>
    <h3>1. Confusing WET with Central European Time (CET)</h3>
    <p>
      Spain (except the Canary Islands), France, and Germany operate on CET (UTC+1), which is 1 hour ahead of WET (UTC+0). Assuming Lisbon is on CET leads to a 1-hour scheduling error.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>2. Conflating EST with Eastern Daylight Time (EDT)</h3>
    <p>
      EST is UTC-5 (winter), while EDT is UTC-4 (summer). Always check if North America is currently observing EST or EDT on your query date.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>3. Azores Island Offset Oversights</h3>
    <p>
      The Azores archipelago operates on Azores Time (AZOT, UTC-1), which is 1 hour behind mainland Portugal (WET). Always verify whether your Portuguese contact is in Lisbon or the Azores.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>4. Date Boundary Rollover Errors</h3>
    <p>
      Converting 02:00 AM WET to EST yields 9:00 PM EST on the previous calendar evening. Failing to account for the date shift causes calendar invitation mistakes.
    </p>
  </article>
</section>

<!-- TECHNICAL TIMEZONE DATABASE SECTION -->
<section class="content" id="technical-details" aria-labelledby="tech-heading">
  <h2 id="tech-heading">Technical Specifications & IANA Timezone Database Metadata</h2>
  <p>
    Software engineers and system architects require canonical IANA database identifiers for reliable timezone math.
  </p>
  <div class="method-grid">
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>Canonical Identifiers</h3>
      <p>The primary IANA time zone key for WET is <code>Europe/Lisbon</code>. Alternative aliases include <code>Atlantic/Madeira</code> and <code>Atlantic/Canary</code>.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>ISO 8601 Offset Format</h3>
      <p>WET is expressed in ISO 8601 strings as <code>+00:00</code> or <code>Z</code> (e.g., <code>2026-07-30T14:00:00Z</code>).</p>
    </article>
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>POSIX Timezone Specification</h3>
      <p>The standard POSIX string for Portuguese time is <code>WET0WEST,M3.5.0/1,M10.5.0/2</code>, defining March and October transitions.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>Network Time Protocol Sync</h3>
      <p>NTP servers in Portugal synchronize against atomic time standards maintained by the Faculty of Sciences of the University of Lisbon (FCUL).</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

